        "spatangoida": "Spatangoida",
        "spatangoida-περιγραφή-1": "Spatangoida, or heart urchins, are a group of irregular echinoids adapted for life buried within marine sediments. Unlike regular sea urchins, they show bilateral symmetry, with the mouth at the front and the anus at the rear—an arrangement suited for a burrowing lifestyle.",
        "spatangoida-περιγραφή-2": "Their test is heart-shaped and dome-like, with petaloid ambulacra on the dorsal surface. Spines and tube feet are specialized for moving through sediment and collecting organic particles, making them effective deposit feeders.",
        "spatangoida-περιγραφή-3": "First appearing in the Cretaceous, spatangoids have a long fossil record. They differ from sand dollars (Clypeasteroida) by retaining a more three-dimensional form and relying on different mechanisms for burrowing and feeding.",
        
        "spatangoida-ετυμολογία-1": "“Spatangoida” from Greek σπατάγγης (spatángēs, an Aristotelian term for a type of sea urchin, possibly the heart urchin) + suffix -οειδής, i.e. “spatangos-shaped”. The order was established by Claus in 1876.",
        

